            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent admission of your 89-year-old male patient, who presented with symptomatic aortic valve regurgitation. On admission, clinical examination revealed signs of volume overload with peripheral oedema and reduced exercise tolerance. Diagnostic workup including electrocardiography, chest X-ray, blood tests, and transthoracic echocardiography confirmed severe aortic regurgitation with preserved left ventricular function. Given the patient's advanced age and overall frailty, a multidisciplinary discussion concluded that surgical intervention carried prohibitive risk. Therefore, a conservative medical management strategy was adopted. The patient was commenced on optimised vasodilator therapy and diuretics, achieving symptomatic improvement. He was discharged in a stable condition with close outpatient follow-up recommended. Please do not hesitate to contact me should you require further information.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
